      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-06-16">June 16, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">A LinkedIn Recruiter Got a Developer to <code>npm install</code> a Backdoor. We Scanned the Repo.</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            The malicious GitHub repo used a <code>prepare</code> lifecycle script to execute arbitrary remote commands the moment you ran <code>npm install</code>. The dependencies looked clean. The attack was already in the repo.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            Today&rsquo;s HN front page: a developer <a href="https://roman.pt/posts/linkedin-backdoor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wrote up a social engineering attack</a> that used LinkedIn to deliver a npm backdoor. A fake recruiter — using a real arts journalist&rsquo;s stolen profile — reached out about a full-stack role. The job task: clone a GitHub repo and &ldquo;check out the deprecated Node modules issue.&rdquo;
          </p>

          <p>
            The repo looked legitimate. 39 commits attributed to a real developer whose GitHub history appeared authentic. A React frontend, a Node backend. Standard stuff.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trap was in <code>package.json</code>:
          </p>

          <pre class="code-block"><code>"scripts": {'{'}
  "prepare": "app:pre",
  "app:pre": "node app/index.js"
{'}'}</code></pre>

          <p>
            npm&rsquo;s <code>prepare</code> lifecycle script runs automatically after <code>npm install</code>.
            The <code>prepare</code> hook called <code>app:pre</code>, which invoked <code>node app/index.js</code>.
            The payload &mdash; 250 lines of code hidden in <code>app/test/index.js</code>, disguised as test infrastructure &mdash;
            assembled a URL from fragmented strings and executed whatever the server sent back.
          </p>

          <p>
            The author caught it in seconds using an AI agent in read-only mode. But most developers would have just run the install.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What the dependency profile looked like</h2>

          <p>
            The malicious repo was a React/Node project. We reconstructed the likely dependency profile &mdash;
            the standard packages you&rsquo;d expect in a full-stack interview task &mdash; and ran them through
            <a href="/audit/">Commit&rsquo;s behavioral audit</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="essay-table-wrap">
            <table>
